---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy policy"
  description="What MindDrop collects, what it does not, and who else is involved."
>
  <section class="page policy">
    <h1 class="policy-title">Privacy policy</h1>

    <p class="policy-updated">Last updated 9 August 2026</p>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        MindDrop keeps your content on your own computer. This policy covers the
        few places where that is not the whole story: this website, and the Pro
        features that need a server to work.
      </p>

      <h2>Who we are</h2>

      <p>
        MindDrop is made by [COMPANY NAME], registered at [REGISTERED ADDRESS],
        business ID [BUSINESS ID]. We are the data controller for everything
        described here. For anything to do with your data, write to <a
          href="mailto:[CONTACT EMAIL]">[CONTACT EMAIL]</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>The app</h2>

      <p>
        The free version of MindDrop does not talk to us. Your entries are plain
        files in folders you choose, on your own disk. There is no account, no
        telemetry, and no crash reporting, and the app works with no internet
        connection at all.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>

      <p>
        We use Plausible for analytics. It sets no cookies, stores no personal
        data, and cannot follow you between sites. All we see are aggregate page
        views, referrers, and rough country-level locations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Your theme choice is kept in your browser's local storage. It never
        leaves your device.
      </p>

      <h2>Pro accounts</h2>

      <p>
        Subscribing to Pro means creating an account, for which we store your
        email address and the state of your subscription. We use your email
        address to sign you in and to write to you about your account. We do not
        send marketing email unless you ask for it.
      </p>

      <h2>Payments</h2>

      <p>
        Payments are handled by Paddle, which acts as the merchant of record.
        Your card details go to Paddle and never reach us. We receive only what
        we need to run your subscription: that it was paid, and when it renews.
        <a href="https://www.paddle.com/legal/privacy" rel="external"
          >Paddle's own privacy policy</a
        > covers what they do with it.
      </p>

      <h2>Sync</h2>

      <p>
        Synced content, including any files attached to your entries, is
        encrypted on your device before it is sent, with a key only you hold.
        What we store is ciphertext we cannot read, and neither can anybody who
        obtains it from us. We can see how much space an account uses and when
        it last synced, because the service cannot run without that.
      </p>

      <p>
        Losing your key means losing access to the encrypted copy. We cannot
        recover it for you, which is the other side of not being able to read
        it.
      </p>

      <h2>Publishing</h2>

      <p>
        A published page is a page on the public internet. Anything you put on
        one is readable by anyone with the link, or by anyone with the password
        where you set one, and search engines may index public pages. Unpublish
        it and we take it down, though copies already made by others are beyond
        our reach.
      </p>

      <p>
        Publishing works differently from sync. The entries behind a published
        page, along with any files attached to their properties, are stored in a
        database unencrypted, because a page cannot be served from content we
        cannot read. This applies only to what you publish; everything else you
        sync stays encrypted.
      </p>

      <p>
        We keep ordinary server logs for published pages, including IP addresses
        and requested URLs, for a short period, in order to keep the service
        running and to deal with abuse. If you connect your own analytics to a
        published page, that provider's policy applies to whatever it collects.
      </p>

      <h2>AI features</h2>

      <p>
        When you use an AI feature, the content it needs is sent to a model
        provider to be processed. We use Anthropic and OpenAI, each for the work
        it does best. Both are contractually bound not to train on what we send
        them, and neither retains it beyond what processing the request
        requires. We do not use your content to train anything either.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nothing is sent unless you use a feature that needs it. AI can be turned
        off entirely, and you can supply your own API key or point MindDrop at a
        model running on your own machine, in which case your content goes to
        that provider or nowhere at all.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping and deleting your data</h2>

      <p>
        We keep your account data for as long as your account exists. Delete
        your account and we delete it, along with any synced content and
        published pages, within 30 days. Backups age out on their own within 90
        days. We keep records of payments for as long as tax law requires us to.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>

      <p>
        Under the GDPR you can ask us for a copy of your data, ask us to correct
        or delete it, ask us to restrict what we do with it, and object to that
        processing. Write to <a href="mailto:[CONTACT EMAIL]">[CONTACT EMAIL]</a
        > and we will answer within a month. If you think we have handled your data
        badly, you can complain to your local data protection authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>

      <p>
        If this policy changes in a way that matters, we will say so on this
        page and, for anything significant, email account holders. The date at
        the top is always the date of the current version.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .policy {
    padding-block-start: var(--space-8);
  }

  .policy-title {
    font-size: var(--step-4);
  }

  .policy-updated {
    margin-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-7);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }
</style>
